Smart-Scale “Applause-Tweet” Sketch  
Goal: every time the scale reads a new, lower tenth-of-a-kilogram value (…110.3 → 110.2 → 110.1…) it fires one tweet containing only the clapping-hands emoji repeated once per total kilogram lost so far.

Example  
Start weight: 110.3 kg  
110.2 kg → tweet: 👏  
110.1 kg → tweet: 👏👏  
110.0 kg → tweet: 👏👏👏  
…etc.

Hardware Bill of Materials
- Any digital bathroom scale with an HX711 load-cell amplifier (most cheap scales already contain this).  
- ESP32-S3 or ESP32-C3 (built-in Wi-Fi + BLE, Arduino-compatible).  
- 3.3 V step-down if your scale runs on 4×AAA (6 V).  
- Optional: 3D-printed base to hide the ESP32.

Wiring (inside the scale)
Scale load-cell → HX711  
HX711 VCC → 3.3 V  
HX711 GND → GND  
HX711 SCK → ESP32 GPIO 14  
HX711 DT  → ESP32 GPIO 12  
ESP32 EN/BOOT pins left floating, add RESET button for OTA updates.

Firmware (Arduino core)

```cpp
#include <WiFi.h>
#include <WiFiClientSecure.h>
#include <ArduinoJson.h>
#include "HX711.h"
#include <WiFiClientSecure.h>
#include <base64.h>

// Wi-Fi
const char* SSID     = "YOUR_SSID";
const char* PASSWORD = "YOUR_PASS";

// Twitter v2 (using bearer token obtained from a Twitter developer account)
const char* BEARER = "YOUR_TWITTER_BEARER_TOKEN";
const char* TWEET_URL = "https://api.twitter.com/2/tweets";

HX711 scale;
const int LOADCELL_DOUT_PIN = 12;
const int LOADCELL_SCK_PIN  = 14;

float startWeight = 0.0;   // captured on first power-up
float lastTenth   = -1.0;

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  WiFi.begin(SSID, PASSWORD);
  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) delay(500);

  scale.begin(LOADCELL_DOUT_PIN, LOADCELL_SCK_PIN);
  scale.set_scale(-467.0);     // calibrate this number to your load cell
  scale.tare();                // zero the scale with nothing on it

  Serial.println("Please step on the scale...");
  while (scale.get_units(10) < 10) delay(500); // wait for user

  startWeight = roundf(scale.get_units(10) * 10) / 10.0; // 1-decimal
  lastTenth   = startWeight;
  Serial.printf("Start weight locked: %.1f\n", startWeight);
}

void loop() {
  float w = roundf(scale.get_units(10) * 10) / 10.0;
  if (w < lastTenth && w > 0) {
    int claps = int(startWeight - w + 0.05); // total kg lost
    String emoji = "";
    for (int i = 0; i < claps; i++) emoji += "👏";
    sendTweet(emoji);
    lastTenth = w;
  }
  delay(500);
}

void sendTweet(const String& text) {
  WiFiClientSecure client;
  client.setInsecure(); // quick demo; use certs in production
  if (!client.connect("api.twitter.com", 443)) return;

  StaticJsonDocument<200> doc;
  doc["text"] = text;
  String payload;
  serializeJson(doc, payload);

  String auth = "Bearer " + String(BEARER);
  String req  = "POST " + String(TWEET_URL) + " HTTP/1.1\r\n" +
                "Host: api.twitter.com\r\n" +
                "Authorization: " + auth + "\r\n" +
                "Content-Type: application/json\r\n" +
                "Content-Length: " + String(payload.length()) + "\r\n\r\n" +
                payload;

  client.print(req);
  Serial.println("Tweeted: " + text);
}
```

Installation & Calibration
1. Solder wires from the HX711 to the ESP32 as shown.  
2. Upload sketch via USB-C cable; set Tools → Partition Scheme → “Huge App” (for OTA later).  
3. While serial monitor is open, step on the scale; the first stable reading becomes the baseline.  
4. Adjust `scale.set_scale()` value until serial output matches a known weight.

